<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Prokop the Great</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1380–1434</span> · Also known as Prokop Holý, Procopius the Great</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Prokop the Great was a Hussite priest and military leader, the chief commander of the Taborites after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-zizka">Jan Žižka</a>'s death. He defeated crusading armies at Ústí nad Labem in 1426 and Domažlice in 1431 and led raids into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and Silesia. He took part in negotiations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-basel">Council of Basel</a>, but was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lipany">Battle of Lipany</a> in 1434.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prokop-the-great.jpg" alt="Prokop the Great" width="320" height="407" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prokop_Hol%C3%BD_(Procopius_Rasus)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
